From 24620df7540a0a32bed7b6b8ea18b9d35a89f131 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:41:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: settings to map Dolby Vision as HDR10 to the
 display, and to turn native Dolby Vision off
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two settings in System > Display, next to the other Dolby Vision ones.

"Dolby Vision as HDR10: map to the display" (off by default), with the peak
of the display in cd/m2, its gamut (BT.2020, DCI-P3 D65, BT.709) and the
curve (BT.2390 by default, or libplacebo's spline). It is for projectors
and HDR10 displays that map HDR10 poorly. On, the pictures that are
reconstructed for output as HDR10 are mapped with dvbridge_render_hdr10_mapping(),
and the output is told what describes them then (the display's peak as
mastering maximum and MaxCLL, its gamut as primaries, MaxFALL not known).
The renderer takes the mapping when it is configured
(TakeDVConversionMapping()), so that what the output is told and how the
pictures are drawn stay the same until it is configured again. Since only a
reconstructed picture can be mapped, while it is on every Dolby Vision
stream that is not sent natively is reconstructed (ConversionSetting::EVERY),
whatever its profile, as long as "Dolby Vision as HDR10 when needed" is on;
the setting for profile 7 with an enhancement layer is then implied and
greyed out. The gamut of the mastering display, where the stream's HDR10
metadata gives it, tells libplacebo where the colours can be: a picture
mastered in P3 is then not desaturated for a P3 display.

BT.2390 is the default curve because, measured on the test stream with a
100 cd/m2 display, it keeps the tones from 0.05 to 20 cd/m2 within about 5 %
of the master, where the spline darkens them by about 40 % (libplacebo's
knee adaptation); both are libplacebo's.

"Native Dolby Vision output" (on by default, as before): off, Dolby Vision
plays as on a display without it (as HDR10 where that setting says so,
else as Kodi plays it), and the Dolby Vision page of the player information
says "turned off in the settings". Until now only a kernel parameter
could do that. It is read when a video starts, never during one, and
SupportsNativeDVOutput() gives "settings" as the reason, first of all.

The strings are translated as the other strings of Hades are: the core
strings in English, the same in Estuary (31770-31787, and 31760 for the
player information), translated there into Spanish (es_es with "vídeo",
es_ar and es_mx with "video"), and the settings dialog takes them from
Estuary (CGUIDialogSettingsBase::GetLocalizedString()).

Nothing changes for anyone who keeps the defaults.
